= The Munich Rising

<img src="img/Hoffmann_Johannes.jpeg" style="float:left; margin-right:1em; margin-bottom:0.5em; width:210px; object-fit:cover; object-position:top; border:6px solid #000000ff;"> Ever since the assassination of Kurt Eisner in February, the Bava<span style="color: #0098D4;">ian</span> capital has been a city without a centre, its Landtag paralysed, its SPD Minister-President Johannes Hoffmann clinging to an authority no one obeys, and its streets given over to a ferment of soldiers' councils, striking workers, and <span style="color: var(--uspd-colour);">revolutionary clubs</span>. Word of the <span style="color: var(--kpd-colour);">Soviet Republic</span> proclaimed in Budapest has electrified the <span style="color: var(--uspd-colour);">radicals</span>, who now insist that Bava<span style="color: #0098D4;">ria</span> too can leap straight to council rule. Against a government that cannot command its own soldiers, the pressure for a <span style="color: var(--uspd-colour);">Räterepublik</span> has become irresistible...
